# Godot drag and drop

Every drag in Sonara follows one model: **nothing moves until the mouse is released**. While dragging, the
UI only shows *where* the drop will land, with a glowing indicator that never takes layout space.

Shared pieces:
- @Godot/components/DropIndicator.gd (glow line and header outline; `DropIndicator.place` / `hide_indicator`)
- @Godot/components/DragDrop.gd (`current_drag`, `visible_rect` / `is_point_visible`, `forward_drops`)

Implementations (payload, resolver, owner, test):
- Mixer strips: `mixer/MixerChannelDrag.gd`, `mixer/MixerChannelDropTarget.gd`, `mixer/Mixer.gd`,
  `tests/test_mixer_channel_drop.gd`
- Arranger tracks: `arranger/tracklist/TrackDrag.gd`, `arranger/tracklist/TrackDropTarget.gd`,
  `arranger/tracklist/TrackList.gd`, `tests/test_track_drop.gd`
- Devices (DeviceLane, nested container folders, mixer compact lists): `devices/DeviceDrag.gd`,
  `devices/DeviceDropTarget.gd`, `devices/DeviceChainDropHost.gd`, `tests/test_device_drop.gd`

## Rules

1. **Commit on drop only.** Never `move_child`, reparent, or change model data during the drag. Don't
   build "live reorder, then revert on cancel" logic. A cancelled drag (Escape, invalid target) then
   needs no cleanup.
2. **No placeholders or gaps.** Don't insert spacer or placeholder nodes, and don't grow margins to
   open a gap. Anything that changes layout while dragging makes the whole UI jump.
3. **Indicators are overlays.** Draw them on a `top_level = true`, `mouse_filter = IGNORE` control with
   a raised `z_index`, positioned by `global_position`/`size`. Create it lazily, hide it on
   `NOTIFICATION_DRAG_END` and whenever the target is invalid.
4. **Glowing blue style.** Use the shared look: accent `#5aa0ff` (export it as `drop_indicator_color`),
   a bright ~3px core with a soft layered halo.
   - *Insert between items*: a glowing line across the gap (vertical for horizontal lists, horizontal
     for vertical lists), placed on the edge of the item the drop lands before.
   - *Drop onto / into a container* (group header, folder, slot): a glowing outline of that header
     with a faint fill. Outline only the header, not the whole item and its children.
   Use `DropIndicator` instead of drawing a new style.
5. **One resolver.** Put "what happens if dropped at this point" in a single
   static `resolve(owner, drag, global_mouse) -> Target` returning a `kind`, the model arguments
   (parent, after-sibling …) and `indicator_rect`. `_can_drop_data`, `_drop_data` and the indicator
   all call it, so the indicator always shows exactly what the drop does. `commit()` lives on the
   target.
6. **Resolve from the global mouse, not `at_position`.** Drops are forwarded from many child controls
   (`set_drag_forwarding`); forward them all to the owner, which resolves with
   `get_global_mouse_position()`. Restrict to the visible pane rect so scrolled-out content can't
   catch drops.
7. **Positions are "after sibling" (null = first)**, computed from item midpoints while skipping the
   dragged item itself, not raw child indices. Dropping into the item's current slot is valid but
   `commit()` returns false (no-op, no history entry).
8. **Nest targets are explicit.** Nesting happens only over a container's header (or its
   fold-out header bar). Anywhere else on the item means "insert beside it".
9. **Only valid targets glow.** Show the indicator only when the resolver returns a valid target for
   the drag in progress. Never light up zones on `NOTIFICATION_DRAG_BEGIN` regardless of drag type.
   Owners that do per-frame work can `set_process()` on DRAG_BEGIN only when the drag data is theirs.
10. **Mark the source in place.** Dim the dragged item with `modulate.a` (never `visible`, which
    changes layout) and restore it from the payload's `drag_completed`. Connect a method, not a lambda,
    because a drop may free and respawn the source.
11. **Go through the model and history.** Commit through the data model / `HistoryUtil`, never by
    editing UI nodes. The UI rebuilds from model signals. Root display order that has no model call
    (e.g. `ChannelsBox.sync_channel_order()`) is the only exception.
12. **Test the resolver headless.** Resolve at points taken from real rects (`header.get_global_rect()`
    etc.), assert `kind`, target args and `indicator_rect`, assert that resolving moved nothing, then
    `commit()` and assert the model.

## Payload pattern

Wrap every drag in a payload object whose lifetime is tied to the preview (Godot frees the preview on
drop, cancel or Escape):

```gdscript
class_name ThingDrag

signal drag_completed(data: ThingDrag)

var source: Control = null
var thing: Thing = null
var preview: Control = null
var did_commit := false

func _init(_source: Control, _thing: Thing, _preview: Control) -> void:
	source = _source
	thing = _thing
	preview = _preview
	if preview:
		preview.tree_exiting.connect(_on_tree_exiting)

func _on_tree_exiting() -> void:
	drag_completed.emit(self)
```

Source control:

```gdscript
func _get_drag_data(_at_position: Vector2) -> Variant:
	if not _drag_handle_hit():        # e.g. only from the header
		return null
	var preview := ThingDrag.make_preview(thing)
	var drag := ThingDrag.new(self, thing, preview)
	set_drag_preview(preview)
	modulate.a = 0.5
	drag.drag_completed.connect(_on_drag_completed)
	return drag
```

Owner (the list/pane that resolves drops):

```gdscript
func _process(_delta: float) -> void:
	var vp := get_viewport()
	var data: Variant = vp.gui_get_drag_data() if vp.gui_is_dragging() else null
	if not data is ThingDrag:
		_hide_drop_indicator()
		return
	var target := ThingDropTarget.resolve(self, data, get_global_mouse_position())
	if not target.is_valid():
		_hide_drop_indicator()
		return
	_indicator().show_at(target.indicator_rect, target.is_nest())

func _can_drop_data(_p: Vector2, data: Variant) -> bool:
	return data is ThingDrag and ThingDropTarget.resolve(self, data, get_global_mouse_position()).is_valid()

func _drop_data(_p: Vector2, data: Variant) -> void:
	var target := ThingDropTarget.resolve(self, data, get_global_mouse_position())
	_hide_drop_indicator()
	data.did_commit = target.commit(self, data)
```

## Godot notes

- Drag starts only after the mouse moves past Godot's drag threshold, so a plain click on the same
  handle can still select.
- `_can_drop_data` runs on the control under the mouse. Use `set_drag_forwarding(Callable(), can, drop)`
  on child containers so every visible part of the owner accepts the drop.
- To accept only some drag types on a sub-area, give that area its own narrow handlers rather than
  the owner's general ones (e.g. the mixer right pane accepts strip drags but not assets).
- A rejected drop bubbles up through `MOUSE_FILTER_PASS` parents (containers) but stops at a
  `MOUSE_FILTER_STOP` control (buttons, knobs). `DragDrop.forward_drops(root, can, drop, skip_group)`
  forwards drops from stopping controls that have no handlers of their own.
- `NOTIFICATION_DRAG_BEGIN` / `NOTIFICATION_DRAG_END` arrive on every node; use DRAG_END to hide
  indicators. `get_viewport().gui_is_drag_successful()` tells whether a drop happened.
- Calling `force_drag()` from `_drop_data` must be deferred.
